00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.679 align:start position:0%
filming for future Barack Obama recently  pledged 8 billion dollars in federal  support for a national network of  high-speed rail trains that can carry  passengers at speeds in excess of 150  miles per hour but the super train  fantasy is a mistake for at least three  reasons we're providing eight billion  dollars in seed money that eight billion  dollars won't come close to funding the  national plan which CNN reports could  cost over five hundred billion dollars  rail projects like most  government-funded extravaganzas have a  funny way of ballooning in cost in less  than two years time for instance cost  estimates for California's plan bullet  trains between Los Angeles and the Bay  Area have increased by a third up to  about forty three billion dollars some  estimates say that by the time the  Golden State project is completed in  2020 it'll cost more than a hundred  billion dollars we're gonna be taking  cars off a congested highways reducing  carbon emissions because high-speed rail  has decided to carry people from city to  city it won't have any effect on urban  traffic congestion which mostly takes  place within a given metro area think  about it  flying from Boston to New York doesn't  reduce inbound traffic at the Lincoln  Tunnel neither will taking a high-speed  train and don't confuse high-speed rail  with magic carpets that run on  newfangled clean energy all but two of  the proposed lines run on diesel Amtrak  is a notorious money pit costing  taxpayers at least thirty five billion  dollars in handouts its per passenger  subsidies averaged about $50 per ticket  a hundred and forty million people  schlep to work each Monday through  Friday  Amtrak carries just 78,000 people per  day and the overseas experience isn't  all that great either only two bullets  one in France and one in Japan have  recovered their capital costs and all of  them require massive ongoing subsidies  from taxpayers if we're looking to  reduce traffic congestion and travel  delays it makes more sense to build more  roads and to increase the number of  airplane flights by modernizing the air  traffic control system bullet trains may  be fun to talk about but don't expect  them to carry you or the United States  into the future any time soon  I'm excited I'm gonna come back down  here and write for reason TV I'm Nick  Gillespie
 

